In article <32EB84E6.3852@karmak.com>, Patrick Cunningham <patc@karmak.com> writes:
> I'm working on a project right now to Interface our OpenVMS-based
> business system to some remote applications over a satellite link.
> The link uses General Motor's PULSAT network and their specs say
> that the asynch connection to their DIU uses X.25.  (we are a
> Digital VAR that sells business systems to the Heavy Truck
> industry here in the US)
> 
> I'm afraid I don't know much if anything about X25 and I need some
> direction on where to begin.  The applications i'm hooking to are
> basically just ASCII terminal emulation where I send a login sequence,
> wait for replys and then upload ASCII data in predetermined formats.
> It's all conversation-based - wait for a string and send a response
> or data stream.  Nothing fancy.
> 
> Can anyone point me to a resource for this?  Is this funtionallity built
> into DECnet-Plus?  Is there some software we can use to do the protocol
> conversion (if any) right on our VMS box?
> 
> This will be running on both VAX and Alpha systems.
> 


You need the PSI ACCESS software. This is the client side of the X25 software
for VMS, the server side normally runs in a dedicated WAN router box. (There
used to be a server for Vax called PSI HOST, but I think it has been
discontinued.)

The PSI ACCESS software for VMS contains a programming interface which you can
you in your own applications to access the X.25 connection.

NOTE: You mention that you're supposed to hook into a terminal emulation. This
makes me believe that what the remote application really offers is NOT X.25 but
rather X.29. PSI ACCESS does NOT give you a programming interface to X.29. It
has an interactive facility (command: SET HOST/X29) which you can use to
interactively connect to the X.29 host.

That said, let me explain: X.29 is a protocol layer built upon X.25. X.25 just
sends a data packet over a point-to-point connection. X.29 deals with all kinds
of terminal-releated parameters, and specifies a lot of different packets
(control packets, data packets, etc) which are to be exchanged between the
terminal and the host side of the X.25 connection. So, X.29 implies X.25, but
if your application tries to connect using X.25 and is not "speaking X.29", the
host will disconnect when it can't establish the X.29 protocol connection.

Check if the remote application is X.25 or X.29. Perhaps they offer both, on
different addresses.

If it really is X.25 and nothing else, fine, you can use PSI ACCESS exactly as
documented in the manual. (Many fine examples included.)

If it really is X.29, you have two options: Either interface your code via a
pseudoterminal to the SET HOST/X29 command, or else implement X.29 yourself.
I've done both, they are both messy. It is possible to get by with a subset
X.29 implementation. Wouldn't trust it in all cases, though.

I think I might have some code fragments somewhere.
Mail me if you're interested, and I'll look harder...

