It seems all the IS people out there betting the house on NT don't have the
same background and qualifications as all IS people who continue to be very
happy and satisfied with OpenVMS.  I bet that most OpenVMS shops employ IS
people with formal computer science degrees, and an understanding of
operating systems, basic networking, some programming and the boolean
framework that glues all of the above.  Whereas I see NT techheads
(self-proclaimed engineers) who "know NT very well" badmouthing OpenVMS
because they don't have the aptitude to understand its features enough to
differentiate between it and NT.

My observation is based on the lack of depth in the arguments I hear from
NT-oriented techies.  People discount the value of formal knowledge of
Computer Science principles needed to evaluate and deploy complex systems,
in favor of hands-on self-acquired and fun training that has made the PC so
popular.  

DIGITAL  has continuously marketed to the well-read well-educated IS
professional, whereas MS  targets the less-qualified of the lot.  Part of
the success of NT is due to the ease at which information is published
(Internet, CD, cableTV)  and the familiarity of THE PROGRAM MANAGER
interface of WIN3.1.  Part of DIGITAL's  failure to grow its installed
market share is the lack of understanding of the value of the above.

BUT  how long can this wave of mis-information go on before the CIO's and
CEO's of the world realize that productivity is NOT up due to WINxxxx? And
that total reliability of their systems is NOT up due to the latest and the
greatest in operating systems?  (Remember the Apple commercial with the two
techies fiddling with a WINTEL pc and there manager claiming that "if you
improve productivity any more we would be out of business"?.  

How long can ignorance dominate in this field of science?  Not until IS
realize that someone wants them to upgrade their production systems every 6
months or they're on there own,  because the 1000 known bugs are resolved
in version X+1.0.  Also independent software developers will hurt when they
have to be on every version of the OS every 6 months.  When CEOs realize
that their hardware is getting obsolete before it's depreciated due to the
fatty OS and apps, they'll start to question the soundness of IS decisions.

I'd say in 1997-1998 IS will awake and realize, as the ancient saying goes,
that "kids cannot rule the world no matter how smart they are."  One cannot
label Physics, Chemistry, Mathematics, and Engineering prociples as archane
ideas and as hinderance to progress.  They are the basic elements for
progress.  

Let's not forget that. 

A. Suleiman



